mido: decode the launchd fault instead of photographing it (0131)

0130 proved the guard is in the kernel -- page 1 of the boot log reads
"MIDO BUILD 0130 pmap-guard=af10 (literal bit 10)" -- and that boot got all
the way to ignition without a demote panic.  launchd still died on its first
unresolvable fault, and this is everything the kernel said about it:

  MIDO UFAULT 1: pid 1 kr 0x1 addr 0x201?1d3462? prot 0x3
                 pc 0x10112205b lr 0x101122020 sp 0x16f167270
                 esr 0x9200004?

kr 0x1 is KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS, so vm_fault() found no mapping at all: this
is not copy-on-write and not a permission problem in the usual sense.  Two
very different things produce that answer and the VM side cannot tell them
apart:

  the hardware is right   nothing is mapped at any level, so launchd
                          computed a wild pointer and the bug is in
                          userspace
  the hardware is wrong   a leftover descriptor in a page table page made
                          the walker believe in a mapping the VM never
                          created, which is the same stale-table problem
                          0129 only half closed

The old line cannot distinguish those, and its numbers do not survive a
photograph: the difference between a translation fault and a permission
fault lives in the low six bits of the ESR, which is exactly what a blurry
photo eats.  So print conclusions as well as values:

  MIDO UFAULT 1: pid 1 transl0 kr 0x1 type 0x3 esr 0x92000044
                 insn 0xf9000120 nent 47
  MIDO UFAULT: addr 00000000.2011d346 pc 00000001.0112205b ...
  MIDO WALK: pmap=0x... min=0x0 max=0x... levels 0..3
  MIDO WALK L0[1] tte=0x...
  MIDO WALK L1[...] tte=0x...

  * the name comes from fault_status_t, which sleh.c already computes;
    transl0..3 and perm1..3 are the two cases above
  * addr/pc/lr/sp are printed as two 32-bit halves, so every token is at
    most eight digits
  * insn is the instruction word at pc: it says whether the store is a
    single register, a pair or an immediate-offset store even though the
    register values are not printable
  * the walk prints every level verbatim, so "L2 is not a table pointer"
    versus "leaf pte present, af=1" is the answer

The build banner moves on as well -- "MIDO BUILD 0131 pmap-guard=af10
fault-decode" -- so a photo of page 1 still says which kernel produced the
log.

Bounded to sixteen reports and four walks: a dying launchd takes a dozen
daemons with it and the console ring holds about 2000 lines.
